  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe primary story Tuesday and Wednesday will be a slow increase\nin temperatures, driven by the return of weak southerly low \nlevel flow as the surface high slides just off to the east. \nWhile these temperatures will be noticeably warmer on the heels \nof our current, well below average values, this will be much \nmore typical of this time of the year with temperatures in the \nmid 80s to low 90s and moderate humidity. Still, it likely won't\nbe quite as humid as it otherwise could be, as we will hang \nonto a modified airmass augmented by local evapotranspiration, \nrather than a southerly feed from the Gulf. \n\nWednesday night though, a slowly digging upper trough will move into \nthe upper Mississippi River Valley, driving a weak cold front south \nand into our northern areas sometime late Wednesday or early \nThursday. While there are typical timing differences among ensemble \nmembers and clusters (deeper trough = faster, stronger front), this \nis not out of the ordinary for this time range, and confidence in \nthe time window described above is growing. Meanwhile, moisture \npooling along this boundary is expected, boosting precipitable water \nvalues perhaps into the 75th-90th percentile per ensemble means,\nin spite of the poor Gulf trajectories. MUCAPE projections \nahead of this front also rise to around 1500-2000 J/kg Wednesday\nafternoon, and drop down to around 500-750 J/kg overnight as \nthe front moves into our area. These parameters are not \nparticularly eye- catching but are supportive of a round of \nshowers and thunderstorms late Wednesday/early Thursday at a \nminimum. So far, there is not much to support higher-end \nthreats, as modest shear/instability and the nocturnal timing \nmay limit the severe ceiling. There are some parts of the \npattern that support locally heavy rain (low level jet impinging\non a slow-moving front, with a large parallel component to the \nmean flow), but moisture parameters are a bit lower than ideal \nfor this scenario, and the front is currently a bit more \nprogressive than you'd typically need. We also do not see a \nnotable signal for heavy rain in long-range indices (such as the\nECMWF Extreme Forecast Index), although there IS a stronger \nsignal just to the north in Iowa Wednesday. For now, it is \nsomething to monitor, but the probability of notable severe \nstorms or heavy rain/flooding remains low overall. \n\nForecast confidence in additional precipitation chances the \nremainder of the week are much lower, but it does appear that the \nfront will stall somewhere in the region, keeping the door open late \nin the week. Meanwhile, the presence of this front will act as a \nspeedbump for our warming trend, which may take a few days to resume \nas upper heights are slow to build. We also note widening ensemble \ntemperature spreads late in the week, in spite of a slow increase in \nensemble means, which indicates that while there is a slight \npreference for warming, additional weak fronts/precipitation may \nslow this down. It's also notable that we are favored to remain in a \nlargely zonal, west-northwest flow regime through the end of the \nweek, which doesn't strongly favor significant warming and also \nleaves the area vulnerable to passing shortwaves.",
